\section{Members that fail}{

Each member is one unit: a failure in any step of its re-tune, its
out-of-fold predictions, its final fit or its test scoring fails that
member alone, and \code{models$results} records the step and the cause in
\code{error_message}, the root cause's class in \code{class}, and, with
\code{control_horizons(keep_traces = TRUE)}, its backtrace as text in \code{trace}.
A member's own warnings, from the engines and from tune, are in
\code{models$results$warnings}. A bug, a refusal or a missing package is not
the member's and stops the call: before any member is fitted, \code{fit()}
refuses members whose model's engine or feature-selection step needs a
package that is not installed, naming each (\code{horizons_error_missing_package}). When the first members fail the same
way (3 of them by default, \code{control_horizons(breaker = )}, \code{FALSE} to
fit every member), and between them span two of the members' models,
transformations, preprocessing methods and feature selections, each of
these the members have more than one of, \code{fit()} stops with
\code{horizons_error_systemic} and the members so far as \code{results}, as
\code{\link[=evaluate]{evaluate()}} does ("Failed configurations" there); and it stops the same
way when a daemon dies (see "Parallelism"). A daemon lacking a package the members need is refused
before the run (\code{horizons_error_missing_package}); one found lacking it
later fails the members it runs with tune's error. With
\code{control_horizons(debug = TRUE)} the re-tunes run in this session and the
first failure, inside tuning included, stops the call with its backtrace.
Before a parallel run \code{fit()} refuses a session loaded with
\code{devtools::load_all()}, and, asking every daemon connected one task each,
daemons that load another version of horizons
(\code{horizons_error_dependency}), since the daemons prepare the members'
folds and tune's tasks fit the members' engines, horizons' own PLS engine
among them, or cannot load a package the members need, their engines' or
their feature selections' (\code{horizons_error_missing_package}). A feature
selection that fails while a fold is prepared fails every member that
reads that preparation, with the step's own class and words.
}

\section{Cold start from one configuration}{

With one configuration there is nothing for \code{evaluate()} to choose, so a
configured \code{horizons_data} whose \code{config$configs} has exactly one row can
go straight to \code{fit()} (#45). \code{fit()} then draws the train/test split
itself, by the draw \code{evaluate()} uses, so at the same \code{seed} it holds out
exactly the rows \code{evaluate()} would have, and applies a response trim
\code{validate()} requested as \code{evaluate()} does, on fences from the training
partition alone. With no parameters from \code{evaluate()} to keep, \code{fit()}
tunes the configuration as \code{evaluate()} would, since this is its only
tuning: a space-filling grid of \code{grid_size} points, then a Bayesian search
of \code{bayesian_iter} iterations (\code{\link[=configure]{configure()}}), raised to ten points a
tuned parameter and stopped by \code{control_horizons(no_improve = )} as in
\code{evaluate()}, keeping the candidate with the lowest CV RMSE. At
\code{bayesian_iter = 0} it grids only, as \code{evaluate()} does. A cold start does
not read \code{final_bayesian_iter}, the budget of the re-tune of \code{evaluate()}'s
members: one above 0 is ignored, with a warning of class
\code{horizons_warning_input}. The console tree's header and a note on the log
say what the search ran, and \code{models$results$warm_start} is \code{FALSE}. On
either path, a search space
whose tuned parameters are all whole numbers, costing no more fits than
the grid and the Bayesian iterations together, is gridded whole with no
Bayesian search, as \code{evaluate()} grids it: a submodel's values share one
fit, so that is every plsr member, each \code{num_comp} up to its cap tried
from one fit a fold. On the \code{evaluate()} path that holds only with the
re-tune on; off, as it is by default, each member keeps \code{evaluate()}'s
hyperparameters (see "The re-tune"). The degradation
check needs
nothing from \code{evaluate()}, since it compares the test RPD with \code{fit()}'s
own cross-validation.

The fit carries an unscreened evaluation record in place of
\code{evaluate()}'s: \code{evaluation$screened} is \code{FALSE}, the one configuration is
\code{best_config} with status \code{"not_evaluated"}, its metric and \verb{cv_*} columns
are \code{NA}, and \code{runtime_secs} is 0. \code{evaluation$recipe} records the recipe
settings as \code{evaluate()} does, and \code{fit()} applies \code{evaluate()}'s checks
first: an outcome that is not constant, a Savitzky-Golay window narrower
than the spectrum, and more rows than \code{cv_folds} to draw the folds from,
which \code{fit()} checks on either path. Re-fitting a cold-started fit starts
cold again. With more than one configuration \code{fit()} aborts with class
\code{horizons_error_input}:
choosing among them is what \code{evaluate()} is for.
}

\section{Parallelism}{

Before any member is fitted, each fold's recipe is prepared once for the
call, for every member that shares its preprocessing and feature
selection (and, for a selection that reads the outcome, its response
transformation), as \code{\link[=evaluate]{evaluate()}} prepares its configurations' ("Parallelism"
there); the preparations are held in this session and dropped when the
call returns. With \code{control_horizons(allow_par = TRUE)} they are prepared
on the daemons, one task each, longest first, each carrying its rows, and
those still running are cancelled when the call stops; both need mirai's
dispatcher, so on daemons set with \code{dispatcher = FALSE} \code{fit()} warns of
it once, with class \code{horizons_warning_input}, as \code{\link[=evaluate]{evaluate()}} does, the
warning a row of the object's \code{log} and "no dispatcher" on the progress
tree's parallel line. A pool left with no daemon while they run stops the
call with \code{horizons_error_no_daemons}, every member still to fit. Then
tune runs the CV folds inside each member's re-tune, when it runs, and its
OOF pass on the \href{https://mirai.r-lib.org}{mirai} daemons the caller has set
(\code{mirai::daemons(4)} before the call); a
\code{future::plan()} is not used, and \code{fit()} never sets, resizes or resets
the daemons. With fewer than two connected, \code{fit()} runs sequentially and
warns, with class \code{horizons_warning_substitution}; the warning is also a
row of the object's \code{log}. A run in this session fits \code{rf}, \code{xgboost} and
\code{lightgbm} members on \code{control_horizons(threads = )} threads, one by
default, and the results are the same on any number; on the daemons each
fits on one, and \code{threads} above one with \code{allow_par} is refused, as in
\code{\link[=evaluate]{evaluate()}}. A daemon that dies during a member, read as
fewer daemons connected after it than before, stops the call with
\code{horizons_error_systemic} and the members so far; so does one exiting
under \code{maxtasks}, \code{idletime} or \code{walltime}, which the count cannot tell
apart, and with fewer than two connected as a member starts (tune would
fit it sequentially) the call stops with \code{horizons_error_no_daemons}. A
pool whose daemons all exit during one of
tune's calls waits inside tune, and a daemon whose library loses a package
after the check before the run fails the member with tune's "Argument 1
must be a data frame or a named atomic vector". \code{fit()} parallelises folds
only in this version; a configs axis across the \code{n_best} members is gated
on the fitted-object memory contract (a fitted object is hundreds of MB
and would cross to workers). Before anything is fitted, a parallel \code{fit()}
checks that the daemons on this machine fit in its memory, as
\code{\link[=evaluate]{evaluate()}} does ("Parallelism" there), warning with class
\code{horizons_warning_input} when they would not.

Each \code{cubist} fit runs in a process of its own, wherever it runs, as in
\code{\link[=evaluate]{evaluate()}} ("Isolated fits" there), so a member's results depend on the
seed alone; a member whose fit's process dies, in its re-tune, its
out-of-fold pass or its final fit, fails with
\code{horizons_error_process_died}. \code{control_horizons(isolate = FALSE)} fits it
in the process that runs it instead, which the tree and the \code{log} say.
}

\section{Outcome range}{

Every prediction \code{fit()} scores (out-of-fold, test and calibration) is
clamped to \code{configure()}'s \code{outcome_range}, as \code{predict()} clamps what it
serves. Before anything is drawn or fitted, on either path, \code{fit()} aborts
with class \code{horizons_error_input} when an observed outcome lies outside
the range or is infinite, naming \code{outcome_range} and how to set it (#76);
when the ranking metric is \code{"rrmse"} and the range's lower bound is
negative, since the mean outcome it divides by can then be zero or
negative; and, on the \code{evaluate()} path, with class \code{horizons_error_input},
when the range differs from the one \code{evaluate()} recorded on its results
rows, since the members were chosen on predictions clamped to that range.
}

\section{Zero outcome variance}{

On either path, before anything is drawn, \code{fit()} aborts with class
\code{horizons_error_input} when every observed outcome has the same value, as
\code{evaluate()} does. It also aborts, before any model is fitted, when the
modelled rows vary but the rows its models are fitted on do not, because
the rows with another value all fell in the test set or the calibration
set, or among the training rows a response trim left out. Every member
would otherwise be fitted to a constant. The message names the outcome,
the value, how many rows have it and where the other rows went (#132).
}

